<div class="article-body">

<p>I spent $15 on a quart mason jar, some net cups, and clay pebbles. Three months later, I had a two-foot tall basil plant that I couldn't give away fast enough. My neighbors stopped buying basil at the store.</p>

<p>That was my first experience with the Kratky method. It's the simplest way to grow food without soil. No pumps, no electricity, no moving parts. You fill a container with nutrient water, put a seedling in a net cup, and walk away. That's it.</p>

<p>This guide covers exactly what the Kratky method is, how to set it up for under $15, and the mistakes I made so you don't repeat them.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-kratky">1. What Is the Kratky Method?</h2>

<p>The Kratky method is a passive hydroponic technique invented by Dr. Bernard Kratky at the University of Hawaii in 2009. Unlike most hydroponic systems that need aquarium pumps and air stones, the Kratky method uses zero electricity. The plant creates its own oxygen supply as it drinks the water.</p>

<p>Here's how it works. You fill a container with nutrient-rich water. You suspend a plant in a net cup above the water so the bottom of the roots touch the liquid. As the plant drinks, the water level drops. An air gap forms between the water surface and the roots. The roots in that gap absorb oxygen directly from the air. The submerged roots keep taking up nutrients.</p>

<p>The plant literally creates its own perfect balance of water and oxygen as it grows. No pumps needed.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Why this matters for beginners</div>
  <p>The Kratky method removes the two biggest barriers to hydroponics: cost and complexity. You can start for $15. If you fail, you're out $15. If you succeed, you'll have fresh herbs and lettuce for the next 3 months.</p>
</div>

<p>I've built DWC systems with air pumps, NFT systems with water pumps, and wick systems with capillary action. The Kratky method is the only one I'd recommend to someone who's never grown anything hydroponically.</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">2. The Science Behind Passive Hydroponics</h2>

<p>The reason the Kratky method works comes down to root physiology. Plant roots need two things: water (to absorb nutrients) and oxygen (to breathe). In soil, tiny air pockets in the dirt provide oxygen while the soil holds moisture.</p>

<p>In a Kratky system, the roots naturally split into two zones. The lower roots stay submerged and specialize in nutrient absorption. The upper roots sit in the air gap and become "air roots". they absorb oxygen directly from the atmosphere.</p>

<p><strong>The key insight:</strong> Don't refill the container all the way. The air gap is the entire point of the system. If you top off the water to the brim, you drown the air roots and suffocate the plant.</p>

<p>This is the most common mistake beginners make, and I cover it in detail below.</p>

<h2 id="starter-setup">3. My Exact $15 Starter Setup</h2>

<p>This is the exact setup I used for my first Kratky grow. Every item has been tested by me.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The $15 Kratky Starter Kit</div>
  <p>• <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=wide+mouth+quart+mason+jar+set&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wide mouth quart mason jar</a> ($3 at any grocery store)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=2+inch+net+cups+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">2 inch net cups</a> ($8 for a 10 pack on Amazon)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroton+clay+pebbles+small+bag&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Small bag of hydroton clay pebbles</a> ($15, but you'll use it for years)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+FloraSeries+starter+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">General Hydroponics FloraSeries nutrient sample pack</a> ($10, lasts 2 months for one jar)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=rockwool+starter+cubes+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Rockwool starter cubes</a> ($5 for 50. Use one)<br>
  • Basil or lettuce seeds ($3)</p>
</div>

<p><strong>Step by step setup:</strong></p>

<ol>
  <li>Soak a rockwool cube in pH-balanced water for 15 minutes.</li>
  <li>Drop 2 to 3 seeds in the pre-cut hole of the rockwool cube.</li>
  <li>Place the rockwool cube in a net cup. Surround it with clay pebbles to hold it in place.</li>
  <li>Fill the mason jar with nutrient solution mixed according to the FloraSeries chart. The water level should just touch the bottom of the net cup.</li>
  <li>Put the net cup in the jar lid opening. The roots should barely reach the water.</li>
  <li>Place the jar under a <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">cheap LED grow light</a> and wait.</li>
</ol>

<p>That's it. No pumps. No timers. No electricity except the grow light.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Pro tip: black out the jar</div>
  <p>Clear glass lets light hit the nutrient water, which grows algae. Wrap the jar in duct tape, aluminum foil, or dark fabric. Algae steals nutrients from your plant and smells bad. A $1 roll of duct tape solves this.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="best-plants">4. Best Plants for Kratky. What to Grow First</h2>

<p>Not all plants work with the Kratky method. The key is fast-growing, leafy crops with short life cycles. Here's what I've had success with and what to avoid.</p>

<p><strong>Grow these first:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lettuce</strong>. The undisputed champion of Kratky growing. Black Seeded Simpson, Butterhead, Romaine. Ready in 30 days from seed.</li>
  <li><strong>Basil</strong>. Sweet basil grows like a weed. My first plant reached 2 feet in a quart jar. Start with 4 jars and you'll never buy basil.</li>
  <li><strong>Kale</strong>. Dwarf varieties work great. Harvest outer leaves for 2+ months.</li>
  <li><strong>Swiss Chard</strong>. Bright lights variety is beautiful and bulletproof.</li>
  <li><strong>Mint</strong>. Almost impossible to kill. Grows fast.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Skip these until you have experience:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Tomatoes</strong>. They need more nutrients than a small Kratky jar can provide. Use a bigger container (5+ gallons) or try DWC.</li>
  <li><strong>Peppers</strong>. Long growth cycle depletes nutrients before harvest.</li>
  <li><strong>Strawberries</strong>. They need consistent moisture levels that a passive system can't maintain.</li>
</ul>

<p>The rule is simple: if it's leafy and grows fast, Kratky works. If it fruits and takes months, use a different method.</p>

<h2 id="mistake">5. The #1 Mistake That Kills Kratky Setups</h2>

<p>I made this mistake on my second jar. The first one grew perfectly. The second one got overwatered. in a hydroponic system.</p>

<p><strong>The mistake: refilling the jar to the top.</strong></p>

<p>Here's what happens. Your plant is growing well. The water level drops. You see the jar is half empty. You think "I should top it off." You fill it back to the brim. Now the roots that were happily breathing in the air gap are fully submerged. They suffocate. The plant wilts. You panic and add more water. It dies faster.</p>

<p><strong>The fix is simple:</strong> Don't refill until the water level is within 1 inch of the bottom of the net cup. And even then, only refill to about halfway up the jar. Leave that air gap. The plant will tell you when it needs more water. look for drooping leaves (not wilted, just slightly less perky).</p>

<p><strong>Second mistake: ignoring pH.</strong> I learned this one the hard way when my basil leaves turned yellow for no obvious reason. The pH was 7.5. Plants absorb nutrients only between pH 5.5 and 6.5 in hydroponics. Outside that range, the nutrients exist in the water but the plant can't take them up.</p>

<p>Get a simple <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+test+drops+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH test kit</a> or digital pH meter. Test every time you mix fresh nutrient solution. A $10 pH meter pays for itself in one grow cycle.</p>

<p><strong>Third mistake: clear containers.</strong> Already covered above. Light + nutrient water = algae. Block the light.</p>

<h2 id="scaling-up">6. Scaling Up: From Mason Jar to 5-Gallon Bucket</h2>

<p>Once you've successfully grown a few jars of lettuce and basil, you'll want more. The Kratky method scales up easily.</p>

<p>The upgrade path is simple: bigger container, bigger plants, larger net cup. Here's what I use now:</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The $50 Kratky Bucket Upgrade</div>
  <p>• <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5+gallon+food+grade+bucket+black&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5 gallon black food-grade bucket</a> ($10 at Home Depot)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=3+inch+net+cups+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3 inch net cup with lid adapter</a> ($5)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroton+clay+pebbles&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">More hydroton clay pebbles</a> ($15)<br>
  • <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+FloraSeries+quart&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Nutrients in quart size</a> ($25, lasts 6 months)<br>
  • <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">LED grow light panel</a> ($55 for a 100W equivalent)</p>
</div>

<p>With a 5-gallon bucket, you can grow kale plants that last 4+ months, multiple heads of lettuce simultaneously, or cherry tomatoes. The larger water volume means the nutrient supply lasts longer and pH stays more stable.</p>

<p>I have four 5-gallon buckets on a wire shelf in my kitchen. Each bucket grows one large plant or 2 to 3 small lettuce heads. Total cost for the shelf setup: about $200. Grocery savings: about $80/month in organic produce.</p>

<p>If you want to learn more about nutrients for larger setups, read our <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">Hydroponic Nutrient Guide</a>.</p>

